 14_7	A third limit. Sometimes there is no way across at all. This series adds z, z squared, z to the fourth, z to the eighth, and on, doubling the power each time. It converges inside the unit circle, but near a dense set of points on the edge it blows up. No continuation can cross the circle anywhere.
 14_8	When a continuation does exist, a formula usually does the work. Gamma's integral only works to the right of the vertical line through zero. But gamma of z equals gamma of z plus one, divided by z. Use that to step left, one strip at a time, and each step adds a new pole, at zero, minus one, minus two, and on.
 15_1	Back to the bump on the real line. Its recipe stopped at one because of two points we could not see from the line, at i and minus i.
-15_2	Everything since came from one demand. The complex derivative must be the same from every direction. On an open region, that makes every small piece of the map a stretch and a turn. It lets boundary values decide the inside. It lets a loop count zeros and poles, and it turns a real integral into a sum of residues. It allows at most one extension to a given connected region, though not always everywhere, and along different paths a continuation can branch.
+15_2	Everything since came from one demand. The complex derivative must be the same from every direction. On an open region, that makes every small piece of the map a stretch and a turn. It lets boundary values decide the inside. It lets a loop count zeros and poles, and it turns a real integral into a sum of residues. It allows at most one extension to a given connected region, though there may be none, and along different paths a continuation can branch.
 15_3	One question to take with you. Centre the bump's recipe at x equals three. How far will it reach?
 15_4	To the poles at i and minus i, which are the square root of ten away. About three point one six in each direction.
